WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.672
In many respects, Benedict

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.441
the 16th was the pope who got a bad rap

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:12.912
nearly 24 years

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:13.813
as Pope John Paul,

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:15.915
the second doctrinal enforcer.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.217
And a German childhood

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.651
lived in the shadows of Hitler's

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:19.919
Nazi regime.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.421
He was often on flatteringly

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.523
referred to as God's Rottweiler

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.125
or the Panzer Cardinal.

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.026
Years of negative

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:26.659
publicity

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.461
caused many of his accomplishments

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:29.896
to go unnoticed

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:32.132
He was the first pope to go green,

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:32.999
making the Vatican

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.267
the first country in the world

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.569
with a zero carbon footprint,

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.805
putting solar panels on its rooftops

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.341
and bringing in electric vehicles,

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:43.977
including a hybrid popemobile.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.178
He was cardinal and pope

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:45.845
during the years

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.013
when the Catholic Church's sex

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.782
abuse scandals came to light.

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:49.749
And he spearheaded

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.518
the Vatican's efforts toward a zero

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.286
tolerance policy.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.355
It was too little, too late for some,

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:57.057
but it was Cardinal Ratzinger

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.291
who organized the Vatican's

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.859
response to the scandal

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.928
including creation of a special office

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.930
at the Vatican to handle cases

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.532
extending and in some cases

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:07.467
eliminating the statute of limitations

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.635
for victims.

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:11.438
And as Pope publicly apologizing,

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:15.208
I am deeply sorry for the pain

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.144
and suffering the victims have endured.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.013
And their shoes.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:20.914
And since,

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:24.884
I suppose I had to share in suffering.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.454
One of his first acts after being elected

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:29.222
pope was to condemn a popular

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.356
church figure, Father

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.425
Marcel Maciel, for sex abuse,

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:34.494
something which had not happened sooner

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:35.361
because of Markle's

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:37.097
close friendship with Pope John Paul.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.164
The second

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:38.998
The high profile

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.800
condemnation Defuzing concerns

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.203
that Benedict wanted to cover up scandal.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.171
However, after his retirement,

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.273
he suffered a reputational blow

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.242
when a church commissioned report found

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:49.809
he knew

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:50.910
about allegations

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.078
and failed to act

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:53.847
against a pedophile priest

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:55.582
while he was archbishop in Munich.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.417
40 years ago,

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.586
Benedict denied the allegations

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:00.820
and the surprise

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.822
for which he will go down in history.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.657
Being the first pope in modern times

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:05.859
to resign

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:06.226
showed that

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:07.160
the man everyone

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.795
thought of as a traditionalist

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.096
was unafraid of bucking

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:12.365
the biggest tradition of them all

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.634
that a pope doesn't step down.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.971
It is me, as in graduation data too

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:19.038
true to form.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.640
He made the historic announcement

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.875
in Latin,

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:22.242
saying

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.543
he lacked the strength of mind

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:25.712
and body to continue as pope.

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:27.780
But this tough traditionalist

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.649
was also a softie.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.850
He loved cats.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.685
Chico and Contrasena were two to

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.522
and classical music.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:36.556
Mozart's Clarinet

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.558
Concerto was a particular favorite

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.094
within the Catholic Church.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:41.761
Pope Benedict

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.030
was a towering intellectual figure,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.565
one of the last to have attended

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:46.900
the historical church council.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.102
Vatican two in the 1960s.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:51.037
He authored hundreds of books

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:51.804
and articles

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.240
and was as much a hero to conservatives

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.643
as a controversial figure for others.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:57.443
A famous

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.979
speech in Regensburg in 2006

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.248
that Benedict said was misinterpreted.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:03.249
Questioning the link

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.918
between Islam and violence

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.819
sparked protests against the Pope

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.490
from some in the Muslim world.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.491
His resignation

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.626
introduced the biggest change of them all

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.995
an era of two living popes

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.433
Although he vowed to retire to a life

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.834
dedicated to prayer

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.836
at a monastery inside the Vatican,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.004
the pope emeritus

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.739
continued to exert influence

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.741
during the Francis papacy,

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.943
weighing in on debates

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:32.045
over sexual abuse and celibacy.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.813
By writing articles and books

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.949
well into his nineties,

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.084
charismatic a elder,

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.919
he had retired as pope,

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.688
but not as a major player

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.323
in the Catholic Church,

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.558
an influence likely to continue

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.594
even after his death.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.429
Father Reese, let me ask you this, your

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:52.565
just initial reaction to the news of Pope

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.467
Benedict's passing.

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.403
Well, of course, it's not a surprise Pope

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:58.771
Francis announced

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:00.473
earlier

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:00.673
in the

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:01.274
week that

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:04.744
the pope that Benedict was very sick

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.079
and declining

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:06.913
and so

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.681
we knew that this was going to happen.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:11.317
But it's still a time to mourn

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:12.819
his passing,

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.455
but also to celebrate his ministry

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.391
of service. To the church.

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.692
And Delia,

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:21.594
just want to get your perspective

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.897
in terms of what Pope Benedict's legacy

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:26.499
is to you.

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:30.370
Well,

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.505
personally, I have known and followed

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.140
Pope Benedict and Cardinal Ratzinger.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.541
I was actually taught theology

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:38.411
by a former student of his.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:39.612
So I have

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:41.014
been fairly deeply involved

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:44.083
with Cardinal Ratzinger theology first.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.119
And then when I came to the Vatican,

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.421
I, of course, met him personally.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.523
And one of the things that sticks out

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:51.891
about Pope Benedict

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:53.559
and when people ask me about him

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.261
is the dichotomy

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:58.264
between his public reputation

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.466
and personally meeting him.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:01.501
He it's very

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:03.736
kind of

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.639
alarming to sort of meet him in person

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.442
when you realize he's such a sort of kind

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:13.413
and humble and a person of a great

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.616
intellectual and culture full breadth

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:20.386
so that he had the capacity to focus

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.421
in on you

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:23.823
and say something

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:24.657
about whatever

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:25.758
you were talking about,

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:27.093
which you hadn't

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:29.162
thought of before in a fresh and new way.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:30.897
I think there's very few people

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:32.498
who are original thinkers

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:33.533
in such a way

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:34.867
that Cardinal Ratzinger and Pope

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:35.702
Benedict was.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:36.469
I think that

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:38.004
in terms of his passing,

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.138
it is the end of an era

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.640
for the Catholic Church.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.075
He was the last pope

00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:43.109
and cardinal to participate

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.243
in the Second Vatican Council,

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.912
which was a major event

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.146
in the life of the church.

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.782
And I think that he has

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.952
he is respected by both progressives

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.020
and conservatives

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.688
for his intellectual breadth.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.256
It's what he wanted to do.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.225
A professor, a theologian.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.360
In fact,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.462
he tried to resign several times

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:03.162
under John Paul.

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.464
The second he eventually did

00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:05.598
resign as pope.

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:07.800
This is a man who didn't want to be pope.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.869
He said a few days after he was elected,

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:10.937
when he heard his name

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.839
being called in the Sistine Chapel,

00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:14.040
he felt like he was being led

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.108
to the guillotine.

00:06:15.108 --> 00:06:16.242
This is a man who felt

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:18.177
he was called to be a theologian

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.112
and write his books.

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:21.214
And I think that that is the legacy

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.282
that he leaves to the Catholic Church.

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:25.318
Father, is

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.786
there are many ways that we will,

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:29.021
of course, remember former Pope Benedict.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:31.224
But perhaps for most people,

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:33.292
it will be the fact that he stepped down

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:34.761
something that hadn't been done

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:35.862
in hundreds of years.

00:06:35.862 --> 00:06:36.362
And I'm wondering

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:37.830
just how controversial

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.298
was that at the time?

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.601
Well, it was a big surprise at the time.

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.437
No pope had done this in 600 years.

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:48.508
And the result is that now

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:50.042
future popes are going to find it

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.444
much easier

00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:52.378
to resign

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:55.047
if their health deteriorates to the point

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:55.982
where they can

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:58.418
no longer function in the job.

00:06:58.418 --> 00:06:59.285
This is important

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:00.486
because modern medicine

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:02.822
can keep the body alive

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:05.191
long after a person is capable of

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:08.094
doing such a stressful job as being pope.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:10.029
So it's important.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:13.866
And the fact that he who many consider

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:15.601
a very conservative pope

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.069
was willing to do this

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.372
allows future popes to do it with

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:22.742
with no challenge at all.

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:23.476
And, Delia,

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:24.610
you know, obviously,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.245
we've been hearing comparisons

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:30.249
between Pope Benedict and his successor,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.252
Pope Francis, you know, I guess.

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:34.187
Tell us a little bit

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:36.322
more about their differences and

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:39.058
and how different they are

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:40.226
from each other,

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:42.061
and especially in terms

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:42.995
of their leadership

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:43.896
in the Catholic Church.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:47.800
Well,

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:48.434
in terms of

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:49.035
their leadership,

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:49.435
of course,

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:50.703
they're completely different

00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:51.704
personalities.

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:53.272
I mean, Pope Benedict,

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.141
as I said, was not one

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:56.676
who wanted the job of pope

00:07:56.676 --> 00:07:57.810
because he understood

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.879
that he did not have he was an introvert.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:03.583
He didn't have the ability to meet

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:04.350
a lot of people

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.184
all day to be

00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:06.052
in front of the cameras

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:07.720
to make those gestures of John Paul

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.489
the second or Pope Francis.

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:10.923
They're sort of naturals

00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:11.858
on the world stage.

00:08:11.858 --> 00:08:14.160
Pope Benedict didn't have that

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.128
I wanted to mention

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:18.197
also in terms of his resignation,

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:21.300
that Pope Francis just two weeks ago

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.268
said in an interview

00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.303
that he has already written

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:25.905
a letter of resignation in the case

00:08:25.905 --> 00:08:27.540
that he should become incapacitated.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.242
And I think the resignation of Pope

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:30.877
Benedict had two aspects.

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:31.844
One, it showed

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.446
his great interior strength

00:08:33.446 --> 00:08:34.046
when he spoke

00:08:34.046 --> 00:08:35.314
about his resignation afterwards.

00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:36.315
He said he spoke about

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:37.383
he was very serene about it

00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:39.852
because he spoke about it with God.

00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:41.821
He had a very deep spirituality

00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:43.789
and he was very sure of himself.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.658
In that way was a very courageous move.

00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:47.693
I mean, absolutely floored everybody.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.128
But Pope Francis

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:50.396
just two weeks ago said,

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:50.997
you know,

00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:53.432
I've written a letter of resignation

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:53.966
in the case

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:55.434
that I've become incapacitated

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:56.636
and nobody blinked an eye.

00:08:56.636 --> 00:08:57.003
And that's

00:08:57.003 --> 00:08:58.704
because Pope Benedict

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:02.074
had the courage to do that first, Amara,

00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:05.311
And real quick, Father,

00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:06.345
I was

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:07.280
just thinking on the fact

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.981
that Pope Benedict had to follow

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:10.283
in the footsteps

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:13.352
of the incredibly popular Pope John Paul.

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:14.854
The second and I'm wondering,

00:09:14.854 --> 00:09:15.755
just how difficult

00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:17.790
would that have been for him?

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.793
Well, I think it was difficult,

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:22.728
although they were very similar

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:24.630
in terms of their theology

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.198
and their

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:28.701
policies with regards to the church.

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:30.803
They were very different personalities.

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:33.272
You know,

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.408
Benedict really at heart

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.610
was a German professor.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:42.214
He was he saw his role as a teacher and

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:47.019
this is what he always wanted to do.

00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:49.422
And this is where his stress was.

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:51.190
He wasn't the greatest manager.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:52.925
He did not surround himself

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:55.895
with really excellent people.

00:09:56.963 --> 00:09:59.732
But he was someone who clearly

00:09:59.732 --> 00:10:02.635
articulated the tradition of the church.

00:10:02.902 --> 00:10:04.337
Whether that was convincing

00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:07.974
for everyone or not, his is the question.

